WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.500
MAX SALTONSTALL:
The city of Memphis

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.540
has been frustrated by
potholes in its streets,

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:06.780
especially as they pop up
during the winter freezes.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:10.080
Wet weather plus regular
traffic make it hard to keep up.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.050
Potholes make driving less safe,
damage vehicles on the road,

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.330
and slow down traffic.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:16.680
I'm Max Saltonstall.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.960
And today, we'll
explore how Memphis,

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.150
with some help from
SpringML Google Cloud,

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.160
came up with an
automated scalable system

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.500
to help them combat
their pothole problem.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:29.940
In one year, Memphis repaired
over 63,000 potholes.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.189
So we're talking
about a ton of work

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:34.140
and a huge effort
required to locate them

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:35.670
throughout the city.

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:37.840
To help scale up and
improve efficiency,

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.290
the city could use
data they already have.

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.220
The city buses in Memphis
all have video cameras,

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:46.080
though this rich source of data
wasn't being used as a source

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.150
for detecting potholes.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:51.030
Many cities already have an
assortment of connected devices

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:52.780
and data being collected.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:55.890
So there are problematic
potholes in Memphis

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:58.920
and the need for a better
way to identify them escape.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.380
If they could collect
video from the buses,

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:05.160
detect potholes, somehow
integrate GPS data to locate it

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.250
in space, and then integrate
with city information

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:13.170
service systems, such as
311, to dispatch crews,

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:15.750
but how are they going to do it?

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.160
Machine learning.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:19.320
The city of Memphis
worked with SpringML

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:22.395
to develop a model with Google
Cloud's Video Intelligence API

00:01:22.395 --> 00:01:24.270
that could check the
video footage each night

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:27.450
and store results in Google
Cloud Storage and BigQuery.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:30.750
Then, they made a web
UI for the 311 operators

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:33.180
to view images and
evaluate the results,

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:36.660
deciding on priority for
dispatching the repair crews.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.210
Take a look at what it can do.

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:41.090
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:45.860
The new system
allowed the city teams

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:48.620
to take action quickly and
address the newly detected road

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:50.960
issues within 48 hours.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:54.140
Now Memphis has safer
roads with fewer potholes,

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:56.870
more efficient employees,
lower insurance claims,

00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:59.390
and a modern approach
to service delivery.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:02.060
Automated pothole detection
is just one example

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:05.240
of cities using their data
to augment city services

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:07.452
and improve
government efficiency,

00:02:07.452 --> 00:02:09.410
with the help of cloud
infrastructure and Video

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:11.060
Intelligence solutions.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:12.140
Thanks for joining me.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:14.900
For more information about
SpringML and the Google Cloud

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.180
Video Intelligence API,
check out the links below.

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:19.930
[MUSIC FADING OUT]

